hi this is presh talwalkar in this video  i'm going to teach you the lattice  method of multiplication  let's say you wanted to multiply 12 by  13. we'll draw out a 2x2 table and we'll  write the number 12 in the row and the  number 13 in the column  now we'll multiply the number in the  column by the number in the row so one  times one is one  two times one is two  one times three is three  and two times three is six  now we add up the numbers diagonally so  this becomes first diagonal is six then  two plus three is five and our final  diagonal is just one  and that's our answer of a hundred and  fifty-six  let's do another example of 15 times 21  we'll draw at our table but this time  we'll actually draw in diagonals so  it'll be easier to add up the numbers  when we multiply the numbers we want to  write it as a two-digit number so one  times two is zero two two times five is  ten  one times one is zero one and five times  one is zero five  now we add up the numbers diagonally so  the first diagonal is five the next is  one  the next is two plus one is three and  the final diagonal is zero so this is  zero three one five which we just write  as three hundred fifteen  let's do an example of three digit  numbers we need to draw out a three by  three table because each of the numbers  has three digits  we write the number 526 in the row and  321 in the column  now we multiply each number  in each column by the number of that  particular row  the great part about lattice  multiplication is you only have to know  how to multiply simple things  your times table up to nine  because that's how you can solve for  each box  now we'll add up the numbers  diagonally and finally we make one  adjustment this 18 we need to carry over  the one  so 7 plus 1 is 8 and that gets us to our  answer  we can also use lattice multiplication  for polynomials when we do x plus two  times x plus three we write x plus two  in the row and x plus three in the  column  x times x is an x squared two times x is  two x  x times three is three x and two times  three is six we add everything together  grouping together the x terms and we  have x squared plus five x plus six  i hope you like this video please  subscribe to my channel  you can support me on patreon to get  rewards catch me on my blog binder  decisions on twitter at preshtalwalkar  and you can get my books at amazon
 

